'en_content'=><<<'HTML'
<p>Buying “chorizo” does not always mean buying the same type of food. <strong>Fresh chorizo</strong> and <strong>cured chorizo</strong> can contain similar pork, fat, salt, garlic and paprika, yet the process that follows changes their texture, storage requirements and, most importantly, how they should be eaten safely. The key mistake is not choosing the wrong one for a recipe; it is treating fresh sausage as though it were a ready-to-eat cured product.</p>
<p>The practical rule is simple: <strong>fresh chorizo is raw and must be cooked; cured chorizo has undergone maturation and drying and, when the producer sells it as ready to eat, can be eaten without cooking</strong>. The instructions on the particular product always take priority.</p>
<h2>What is fresh chorizo?</h2>
<p>Fresh chorizo is made from minced meat and fat mixed with seasonings and filled into a casing, without completing the drying process that would turn it into a dry-cured sausage. It retains much more moisture, feels soft and behaves in the kitchen much like seasoned raw minced meat inside a casing.</p>
<p>It is therefore normally refrigerated and intended for the grill, pan, oven, stews or for removing from the casing and crumbling. Before cooking it can look glossy, flexible and very red. Paprika colour <strong>does not mean it has been cooked</strong>.</p>
<h2>What is cured chorizo?</h2>
<p>Cured chorizo goes through fermentation, maturation and drying for a period that depends on its size and style. It loses water, becomes firmer and develops the characteristic aromas of curing. It may be sold in several shapes and calibres.</p>
<p>A cured product labelled ready to eat is normally sliced and served directly. It can also be used in hot dishes, but cooking is not required merely because the product is called chorizo: what matters is whether it is genuinely dry-cured and sold ready for consumption.</p>
<h2>Colour is not the deciding factor</h2>
<p>Both fresh and cured chorizo can be bright red, dark red or somewhere in between depending on paprika, fat and the producer's recipe. Hot versus sweet paprika does not answer the question either.</p>
<p>The decisive difference is <strong>processing</strong>. Fresh chorizo retains a moist raw-meat structure. Cured chorizo has matured and lost moisture. Two products with very similar ingredient lists can therefore require completely different handling.</p>
<h2>How can you tell whether chorizo needs cooking?</h2>
<p>Start with the label. Look for wording such as <strong>fresh sausage, raw product, cook before eating</strong> or specific cooking instructions. If you see any of these, do not taste it raw. If the product is described as dry-cured or ready to eat, follow the producer's serving and storage directions.</p>
<p>If there is no label, for example when buying from a butcher's counter, ask the seller. Appearance alone is not reliable enough.</p>
<h2>Useful texture clues</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Fresh:</strong> usually soft, flexible and moist, with an interior that still resembles raw minced meat.</li>
<li><strong>Cured:</strong> generally firmer, drier and more compact, with slices that hold their shape.</li>
<li><strong>Packaging:</strong> fresh chorizo is usually sold chilled with a relatively short use-by period; cured products often last longer, although some also require refrigeration.</li>
</ul>
<p>These are clues rather than proof. Some cured sausages remain tender and some fresh sausages are tightly filled. Labelling and producer information are more dependable than squeezing the sausage.</p>
<h2>Can fresh chorizo be eaten raw?</h2>
<p>It should not be treated as a ready-to-eat cured sausage. It is raw meat and requires adequate cooking. With thick sausages, moderate heat is often better than fierce heat because it allows the centre to cook before the outside burns.</p>
<p>Repeatedly piercing the casing to “let the fat out” is unnecessary and can make the sausage lose juices. Cook it evenly and follow any specific producer instructions.</p>
<h2>Can cured chorizo be cooked?</h2>
<p>Yes. Ready-to-eat does not mean heat will ruin it. Cured chorizo can add paprika, fat and savoury depth to beans, chickpeas, rice, eggs and stews. Because it already starts dry and concentrated, however, very long cooking can make it tough or release more fat than intended.</p>
<p>If a recipe calls for fresh chorizo and you substitute cured chorizo, expect less moisture, more concentrated seasoning and a different texture.</p>
<h2>Storage is different too</h2>
<p>Fresh chorizo needs refrigeration and its use-by date and storage instructions should be followed closely. Once opened, handle it as other raw meat: minimise time outside the fridge, avoid cross-contamination and cook it within the stated period.</p>
<p>Cured chorizo contains less available moisture and often keeps longer, but there is no universal storage time. Whole versus cut, vacuum packing, temperature and degree of curing all matter. See our guide to <a href="/en/how-long-do-opened-chorizo-salchichon-cured-loin-last-how-to-store/">how long opened chorizo, salchichón and cured loin last</a>.</p>
<h2>Is the casing edible?</h2>
<p>Cooking requirement and casing edibility are separate questions. Fresh chorizo may have an edible natural casing and still require cooking; a cured product can have an outer covering intended to be removed. Our <a href="/en/can-you-eat-chorizo-salchichon-casing-natural-collagen-other-casings/">guide to sausage casings</a> explains the main types.</p>
<h2>The quick rule</h2>
<p><strong>Fresh = seasoned raw meat that needs cooking.</strong> <strong>Cured = matured and dried product that may be ready to eat.</strong> If you are unsure, do not judge by colour or spice level. Read the label or ask the producer.</p>
